---
import DocLayout from '../../layouts/DocLayout.astro';
import BindingMatrix from '../../components/BindingMatrix.astro';
import { contributingHref, demandDrivenBindings, plannedBindings } from '../../data/bindings';
import { withBase } from '../../utils/paths';
---

<DocLayout
  title="Language integrations"
  description="One Rust engine. Explicit capability, qualification, and distribution boundaries."
  back="/"
  backLabel="Cribra"
>
  <p>
    Native and WebAssembly adapters project Cribra Core semantics. They preserve classification
    authority, review-only candidates, UTF-8 coordinates, and explicit transformations without
    creating another detection engine.
  </p>
  <BindingMatrix />
  <h2>Choose a matching profile</h2>
  <p>
    For UniFFI consumers, use one matching generated binding, native library, and profile set per
    process. Kotlin requires one profile JAR per classpath; Swift requires one package exposing <code
      >Cribra</code
    >. Streaming admission and resource limits remain Core-owned.
  </p>
  <h2>Published API reference</h2>
  <p>
    The generated <a href={withBase('/api/0.7.2/swift/core/documentation/cribra/')}
      >Swift Core API reference</a
    > is published for Cribra 0.7.2. It covers the Swift <code>Cribra</code> module for the
    <strong>Core</strong> profile.
  </p>
  <p>
    Flow and Fuse are capability profiles. Fuse is the published official remote package for Swift;
    Core and Flow remain source-build alternatives. Core reference availability does not imply
    complete API coverage for Flow or Fuse, and no Flow or Fuse DocC reference is published. No
    other generated binding has a published DocC reference on this site.
  </p>
  <p>
    See <a href={withBase('/docs/integration-swift/')}>Swift integration</a> for Fuse installation
    and the documented Core/Flow source-build paths. The Fuse package is published; Swift Package
    Index registration has not been submitted.
  </p>
  <h2>Official planned integrations</h2>
  <p>
    These are official workstreams that are planned or in progress. Except where the matrix above
    records otherwise, they are not implemented, qualified or published.
  </p>
  <ul>
    {
      plannedBindings.map((binding) => (
        <li>
          <strong>{binding.name}</strong> — {binding.detail}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <h2>Demand-driven and community candidates</h2>
  <p>
    C#, Go and PHP are planned as demand-driven / community-contribution candidates. They are not
    actively scheduled, and no package or integration is available. Ruby, Elixir and other
    candidates retain the same status. The project will consider them only with demonstrated
    real-world demand or a viable community contribution, and no delivery dates are promised.
  </p>
  <ul>{demandDrivenBindings.map((name) => <li>{name}</li>)}</ul>
  <p>
    Community contributions are welcome under the <a href={contributingHref}
      >contribution guidelines</a
    >. Official support requires an ongoing maintenance commitment, accurate documentation,
    consumer testing and CI coverage. Using a public interoperability surface does not by itself
    make a binding official.
  </p>
  <p>
    See the <a
      href="https://github.com/Metalymph/cribra/blob/main/docs/ECOSYSTEM.md#binding-support-policy"
      >binding support policy</a
    >, <a href="https://github.com/Metalymph/cribra/blob/main/ROADMAP.md"
      >roadmap</a
    > and <a href="https://github.com/Metalymph/cribra/blob/main/docs/INTEROP.md"
      >interoperability contract</a
    > for responsibilities and future qualification gates.
  </p>
</DocLayout>
